## Try it on your PC

No hardware needed: [`examples/host`](examples/host/README.md) runs the driver against a simulated part.
You need git, CMake 3.16+ and a C++20 compiler (GCC 10+, Clang 13+ or MSVC 2022).

1. Get the source:

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/kokeb-labs/driver-template.git && cd driver-template
   ```

2. Build and run the example:

   ```bash
   cmake -S examples/host -B build-example
   cmake --build build-example
   ./build-example/xmpl100_host_example
   ```

3. You should see the driver read the simulated sensor three times:

   ```text
   XMPL100 driver 0.1.0 on the host
   25.00 C
   25.00 C
   25.00 C
   ```

To run the whole test suite instead: `cmake -S . -B build -DXMPL100_BUILD_TESTS=ON && cmake --build build && ctest
--test-dir build`.

## Use it on your MCU

**1. Wire it** ([Hardware](docs/hardware.md) has every pin, the transfer function and the power):

| MCU | XMPL100 | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ADC input | VOUT | An input range that covers 0 V to 1.8 V |
| 3.3 V | VDD | 100 nF to GND close to the pin |
| GND | GND | Same ground as the ADC reference |

**2. Write the port**: a class with `EnsureInitialized()` (bring the ADC up), `ReadVoltageMv()` (one calibrated
conversion) and `DelayUs()`, about 20 lines on most SDKs. [Porting](docs/porting.md) has the contract and a
complete example.

**3. Read the temperature:**

